  "instructions": "Write a concise, natural answer for a traveler using only the selected exact authoring_records. The cards display source flight facts; use prose to explain why a choice is worth considering and its meaningful downside, without reciting the card's route, schedule, points, taxes or seat count. Each concrete option needs one source-resolved reference block: k=p or a for a complete journey, k=l for an incomplete research lead. Put its short decision reason in v and a traveler-friendly label in n. Use k=t only for useful connecting prose. When complete journeys exist, follow Ranking's overlapping time, cost and premium memberships, but describe their actual traveler benefit naturally, such as a shorter trip or a business cabin. One card can serve more than one perspective. Do not announce an unavailable or unselected category, use Time/Cost/Premium as section or card titles, or force a three-part layout. Explain a supported local tradeoff only where it helps the decision. When there is no complete eligible journey, say so once and discuss leads as research directions, never Ranking-style recommendations. Give each lead a distinct conditional next-check reason when supported by program access, cabin, date or a same-program quote difference. A source quote or date alone is a card fact, not a reason. Do not assume the traveler owns program points or prefers a cabin or date, and do not force a quota or a lowest-points rule. A missing full trip price still allows a useful same-program comparison of reported miles or cabin, qualified by the source quote's scope. Do not compare points across programs as economic value or claim cheapest overall from unknown-scope amounts. State shared gaps once after the choices; place only a unique material condition beside its affected choice. Do not repeat generic availability advice; the display adds it separately. Keep any opening sentence to a decision frame, not a summary of every card reason. The k=b block is one SOURC